WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.301 --> 00:00:03.302
-Eddie! Oh, look at you.
-Hey, Jimmy, how are you?

00:00:03.302 --> 00:00:06.606
-Last time I saw you, by the
way, you and your wife just had

00:00:06.606 --> 00:00:09.408
another baby, so now
you have two kids now.

00:00:09.408 --> 00:00:10.977
How old are they now?

00:00:10.977 --> 00:00:14.780
-So, we have Iris, who's 4,
and Luke, who is 2.

00:00:14.780 --> 00:00:19.085
-Geez. And where have you been
for the last few months?

00:00:19.085 --> 00:00:20.853
-Do you know,
we've been really lucky.

00:00:20.853 --> 00:00:24.891
We've been -- Fortunately
everyone has remained healthy.

00:00:24.891 --> 00:00:27.026
Until I just started
shooting again,

00:00:27.026 --> 00:00:30.029
we were in the countryside,
which was wonderful.

00:00:30.029 --> 00:00:33.066
There was space
for the kids to play.

00:00:33.066 --> 00:00:37.537
We basically -- We sort of
became like an animal sanctuary

00:00:37.537 --> 00:00:39.973
while we were there,
which was --

00:00:39.973 --> 00:00:41.674
-A lot of people have done this.

00:00:41.674 --> 00:00:43.209
I have a lot of friends
that have done this.

00:00:43.209 --> 00:00:46.579
Did you get, like -- Did you
get -- Did you get chickens?

00:00:46.579 --> 00:00:49.315
-I mean, we are basically
a parody of lockdown.

00:00:49.315 --> 00:00:53.419
We do have chickens.

00:00:53.419 --> 00:00:56.356
We have five chickens
whom my daughter named.

00:00:56.356 --> 00:00:59.926
They're called Belinda,
Sally Henny Penny,

00:00:59.926 --> 00:01:02.594
Blanket, Flower, and Henry.

00:01:02.594 --> 00:01:04.831
-[ Laughs ]
-And they arrived,

00:01:04.831 --> 00:01:07.133
and they had come from -- they
were sort of battery chickens,

00:01:07.133 --> 00:01:10.470
so they didn't look that well,
but we kind of nurtured them

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:12.538
to health,
and I spent most of lockdown

00:01:12.538 --> 00:01:14.940
running into other people's
gardens, trying to sort of

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:17.777
retrieve chickens
that were causing havoc.

00:01:17.777 --> 00:01:18.978
-[ Laughs ] That's wild.

00:01:18.978 --> 00:01:20.647
-My wife took
great satisfaction from it

00:01:20.647 --> 00:01:25.552
because she basically on day one
kind of just bought me a drill

00:01:25.552 --> 00:01:27.420
and gave me a drill,
and just kind of said,

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:29.822
"Here. Learn how to use that.
Build me a chicken hutch."

00:01:29.822 --> 00:01:32.959
And so I built a chicken hutch,
which is like a triumphant --

00:01:32.959 --> 00:01:34.961
-You built it yourself?

00:01:34.961 --> 00:01:36.129
-I did build it.

00:01:36.129 --> 00:01:39.298
I mean, it was kind of
flat-packed, so I --

00:01:39.298 --> 00:01:42.035
and I succeeded in
kind of the bit

00:01:42.035 --> 00:01:44.103
where the air
was meant to come in.

00:01:44.103 --> 00:01:46.238
I got a bit wrong,
so I ended up having to drill

00:01:46.238 --> 00:01:50.110
holes around it, but genuinely
the amount of pride

00:01:50.110 --> 00:01:51.510
having built a chicken hutch
gave me

00:01:51.510 --> 00:01:53.345
was sort of overwhelming.

00:01:53.345 --> 00:01:55.815
-I just have pride
because I own a drill.

00:01:55.815 --> 00:01:57.417
I've never used it,
but I own it.

00:01:57.417 --> 00:01:59.385
I go, "I know where it is."
-I completely agree.

00:01:59.385 --> 00:02:00.954
-I can tell you
exactly where it is.

00:02:00.954 --> 00:02:02.755
I think I have to charge it up
before I use it,

00:02:02.755 --> 00:02:04.957
but the fact that I own it,
I go,

00:02:04.957 --> 00:02:06.893
"If you ever need me to drill
anything, honey."

00:02:06.893 --> 00:02:08.127
She's like, "I'm good."

00:02:08.127 --> 00:02:09.328
-I've got it.

00:02:09.328 --> 00:02:11.764
The second you get into
drilling, it's just like

00:02:11.764 --> 00:02:13.032
the most satisfying thing
in the world.

00:02:13.032 --> 00:02:14.366
-You want to drill everything.

00:02:14.366 --> 00:02:16.469
-I was sort of walking around,
drilling random holes

00:02:16.469 --> 00:02:21.307
in random walls just because
it gave me some odd pleasure.

00:02:21.307 --> 00:02:24.443
-Did the chickens --
Do they lay eggs?

00:02:24.443 --> 00:02:27.080
-The chickens --
You know, they started off

00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:28.948
not being that keen
for laying eggs.

00:02:28.948 --> 00:02:31.317
They were kind of molting
and not that happy,

00:02:31.317 --> 00:02:34.086
and gradually,
as they kind of fed well,

00:02:34.086 --> 00:02:36.088
they ended up laying
tons of eggs,

00:02:36.088 --> 00:02:37.823
which is great except for
the fact that I loathe eggs.

00:02:37.823 --> 00:02:40.193
I kind of hate eggs.

00:02:40.193 --> 00:02:43.529
So I would sort of have to carry
these eggs in, in the morning.

00:02:43.529 --> 00:02:45.198
Sort of not really wanting --

00:02:45.198 --> 00:02:46.732
but doing that thing you do with
your children.

00:02:46.732 --> 00:02:48.067
-[ Retching ]

00:02:48.067 --> 00:02:51.336
Just take it away.

00:02:51.336 --> 00:02:56.109
-We were adopted by two
stray cats during lockdown,

00:02:56.109 --> 00:02:59.512
as in these two very beautiful
black cats just came and started

00:02:59.512 --> 00:03:06.819
purring at our door, and so
again, my daughter named them.

00:03:06.819 --> 00:03:08.620
One she called Catty Redman.

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:12.025
And then the other one,
who had slightly spiky ears

00:03:12.025 --> 00:03:14.560
and looked a bit terrifying,
she called Scary Catty Redman.

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:17.663
So Scary Catty Redman
and Catty Redman then ended up,

00:03:17.663 --> 00:03:19.532
of course, coming into the house
and everything,

00:03:19.532 --> 00:03:22.401
which is all great, except for
I'm deeply allergic to cats.

00:03:22.401 --> 00:03:28.274
And so this sort of cat allergy,
egg, animal sanctuary thing

00:03:28.274 --> 00:03:32.445
was wonderful in theory but
slightly stressful in reality.

00:03:32.445 --> 00:03:33.813
-Dad of the year.

00:03:33.813 --> 00:03:35.081
I'll give you props for that.

00:03:35.081 --> 00:03:37.884
I could not go near --
I am allergic to cats too.

00:03:37.884 --> 00:03:39.919
-Are you really?
There you go, Jimmy.

00:03:39.919 --> 00:03:41.421
-Yeah. We should be roommates.

00:03:41.421 --> 00:03:43.389
[ Laughter ]

00:03:43.389 --> 00:03:47.059
So you just started a production
on "The Fantastic Beasts."

00:03:47.059 --> 00:03:49.462
-Yeah, yes.
-How is everything going there?

00:03:49.462 --> 00:03:54.200
-Well, we'd done a day of
shooting before COVID-19

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:56.302
really hit the U.K. proper.

00:03:56.302 --> 00:03:59.138
So we started on a Friday and
then on a Sunday night it all --

00:03:59.138 --> 00:04:01.841
we were all told to go home.

00:04:01.841 --> 00:04:04.177
And then just a few weeks ago
we started again,

00:04:04.177 --> 00:04:06.445
and it's a different world,

00:04:06.445 --> 00:04:11.217
but we're so lucky to be back
in work, and it's interesting.

00:04:11.217 --> 00:04:13.753
It's kind of like --
Film crews are amazing people.

00:04:13.753 --> 00:04:16.555
And they're very adaptable
people, and so we have --

00:04:16.555 --> 00:04:18.257
there's a lot of testing.

00:04:18.257 --> 00:04:21.326
There is --
You know, we're in bubbles

00:04:21.326 --> 00:04:25.931
and we're all masked, but it's
been kind of --

00:04:25.931 --> 00:04:28.468
To begin with, I thought
how is this going to be?

00:04:28.468 --> 00:04:31.136
And this ended up being
actually kind of great.

00:04:31.136 --> 00:04:33.473
So it's nice to be back at work.
I feel very lucky.

00:04:33.473 --> 00:04:37.043
-You come on my show normally to
talk about "Fantastic Beasts,"

00:04:37.043 --> 00:04:39.077
but always it's so secretive.

00:04:39.077 --> 00:04:40.346
-Yeah. Very secretive.

00:04:40.346 --> 00:04:42.715
-You're not even allowed
to tell me what it's about.

00:04:42.715 --> 00:04:45.885
But you showed me
a trick or a move before.

00:04:45.885 --> 00:04:50.590
Can you tell us
something about this one?

00:04:50.590 --> 00:04:51.791
-Do you know what?

00:04:51.791 --> 00:04:54.160
Genuinely I'm not being,
like, sycophantic,

00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:56.195
but one of the, like,
joys of promoting

00:04:56.195 --> 00:04:59.999
a "Fantastic Beasts" movie is
getting to come and force you

00:04:59.999 --> 00:05:01.733
to do some
ridiculous dance move.

00:05:01.733 --> 00:05:03.235
It did cross my mind.

00:05:03.235 --> 00:05:05.237
I can't tell you
anything about the new film,

00:05:05.237 --> 00:05:07.673
otherwise I'll -- you know,
someone will get furious,

00:05:07.673 --> 00:05:13.479
but there is a moment which I've
been sort of working on

00:05:13.479 --> 00:05:15.080
and hoping will remain
in the film

00:05:15.080 --> 00:05:18.817
and which I have been trying to
find some sort of

00:05:18.817 --> 00:05:23.055
dinky little thing
that I can teach you to add.

00:05:23.055 --> 00:05:25.924
So I mean, I can't --
I couldn't do it now,

00:05:25.924 --> 00:05:28.560
but I'm hoping that come
November, we'll have another --

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:30.629
we can add one more move.

00:05:30.629 --> 00:05:34.233
We should go dancing one night
and just beast

00:05:34.233 --> 00:05:35.834
those "Fantastic Beasts"
moves out.

00:05:35.834 --> 00:05:37.703
-Deal. I will do that with you.

00:05:37.703 --> 00:05:39.471
Virtual handshake on that, yeah.

00:05:39.471 --> 00:05:40.839
Or elbow bump.

00:05:40.839 --> 00:05:42.675
I would totally do that
with you.

00:05:42.675 --> 00:05:44.043
I will go dancing with you
whenever.

00:05:44.043 --> 00:05:45.678
That's the best.

00:05:45.678 --> 00:05:48.481
I want to talk about
"The Trial of the Chicago 7."

00:05:48.481 --> 00:05:50.449
Can you stick around
and we can talk about that?

00:05:50.449 --> 00:05:51.683
-I would love that, man.

00:05:51.683 --> 00:05:53.919
-Awesome. More with
Eddie Redmayne, everybody.

00:05:53.919 --> 00:05:56.089
♪ -I get the job done ♪

